</tabular>
<p>Find <m>\int_C \mbox{grad } f\cdot d\vec{r}</m>,
where <m>C</m> is</p>
<p><alert>(a)</alert>
A line from <m>(0,2)</m> to <m>(2,4)</m>.</p>
<p><m>\int_C \mbox{grad } f\cdot d\vec{r} =</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert>(b)</alert>
A circle of radius <m>1</m> centered at <m>(1,1)</m> traversed
counterclockwise.</p>
<p><m>\int_C \mbox{grad } f\cdot d\vec{r} =</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>63-58</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert>(a)</alert>
By the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals
<me>\int^{(2,4)}_{(0,2)} \hbox{grad } f\cdot d\vec{r}
    =f(2,4)-f(0,2) = 63 - 58 = 5.</me>




<alert>(b)</alert>
By the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals, since <m>C</m> is a
closed path, <m>\int_C \hbox{grad } f\cdot d\vec{r}=0</m>.





</solution>
